    "pthm-qft-photon-physical-properties": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-qft-free-photon-model",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt the following specified free-photon model in the oriented,\ntime-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime and length coordinates of\n[[def-qft-framework-units]], with metric $(-,+,+,+)$ and $x^0=ct$. The\nclassical electromagnetic input is the smooth source-free branch of\n[[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]; the electromagnetic two-form and its potential\ncomponents are those of [[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], and a supplied\npotential is subject to [[def-em-gauge-transformation]]. These EM suppliers\nare physical input and conventions: they do not by themselves construct a\nquantum representation.\n\nFor almost every $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3\\setminus\\{0\\}$, set\n$\\mathbf n=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and\n$P_T(\\mathbf k)=I_3-\\mathbf n\\mathbf n^{\\mathsf T}$. The mathematical\ndefinition [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]] specifies the positive\none-photon Hilbert space\n\n$$\\mathcal H_T= \\{u\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^3): \\mathbf k\\cdot u(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for almost every }\\mathbf k\\},$$\n\nwith the inherited pairing\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_2=\\int\\overline{u(\\mathbf k)}\\cdot v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k$,\nlinear in the second variable as in [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. The\nvalue assigned to $P_T$ at\n$\\mathbf k=0$ is immaterial because that point has Lebesgue measure zero; no\ncontinuous polarization frame over all momentum directions is chosen. Adopt\nthe bosonic Fock representation $\\mathcal F_s(\\mathcal H_T)$ and the free\nHamiltonian\n\n$$H_\\gamma=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma(|\\mathbf k|),$$\n\nwhere on the $n$-photon sector $d\\Gamma(|\\mathbf k|)$ is multiplication by\n$\\sum_{r=1}^n|\\mathbf k_r|$ and is zero on the vacuum sector. Its exact\nself-adjoint domain and the unitary mass-cone action are those in the\nmathematical supplier [[lem-qft-photon-covariance-spectrum]]. The actual\nsmeared field-strength distributions and bounded Weyl operators are those\nconstructed in [[lem-qft-photon-local-weyl-observables]]; a pointwise\npotential or field-strength operator is not asserted to exist. These\nmathematical suppliers are draft carriers with mixed current Step 1 status: the\ntransverse-space, null-quotient, covariance-spectrum, framework-units, and\nC01 measurement carriers have current ready records; the local-Weyl carrier is\nescalated through its field-strength prerequisite. Readiness records are not\nindependent approval, and every use remains conditional on the exact statement\nand proof supplied.\n\nFor the physical interpretation of an actually constructed state and a\nsupplied normalized measurement instrument, use\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]]: Born probabilities are\nassigned only to that specified model and instrument. This postulate records\nthe model's free Maxwell quanta as photons. It does not assert that the\nmodel has been empirically confirmed, and it introduces no interacting\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space, scattering operator, LSZ limit,\nasymptotic completeness, or nonperturbative continuum limit. In calculations\nusing an indefinite four-polarization auxiliary space, only the positive\ntransverse model or its separately constructed null quotient is physical;\nfour auxiliary components are not four positive photon states.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-photon-transverse-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$$\\mathcal H=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^3),\\qquad \\langle u,v\\rangle_2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\overline{u(\\mathbf k)}\\cdot v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k,$$\nwith the second-variable-linear convention of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]; the real momentum dot product and norm are those of\n[[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Explicitly extend the real dot product\ncomplex-bilinearly to $a\\cdot b=\\sum_{j=1}^3a_jb_j$ for $a,b\\in\\mathbb C^3$.\nEach complex fiber instead has the positive Hermitian pairing\n$\\langle a,b\\rangle_2=\\overline a\\cdot b=\\sum_j\\overline a_jb_j$ and\nnorm $\\|a\\|^2=\\sum_j|a_j|^2$; the displayed integral uses this pairing.\nHilbert completeness follows from [[lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space]]. For $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, write $n(\\mathbf k)=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and\n$$P_T(\\mathbf k)=I_3-n(\\mathbf k)n(\\mathbf k)^T.$$\nAssign any value at $\\mathbf k=0$, which is a Lebesgue-null set. Let $M_{P_T}$ be multiplication by this measurable matrix field and define the positive transverse photon space\n$$\\mathcal H_T=\\operatorname{Ran}M_{P_T} =\\{u\\in\\mathcal H:\\mathbf k\\cdot u(\\mathbf k)=0\\text{ for almost every }\\mathbf k\\ne0\\}.$$\nThe inner product on $\\mathcal H_T$ is the restriction of that on $\\mathcal H$. Each nonzero momentum fiber is the two-complex-dimensional plane $\\{a\\in\\mathbb C^3:\\mathbf k\\cdot a=0\\}$ perpendicular to the real vector $\\mathbf k$ in that Hermitian pairing; no global continuous polarization frame is part of the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-framework-units",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix the oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime and an inertial\nchart of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use signature\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, length coordinates\n$x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres, and coordinate time $t$ in seconds as in\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Greek spacetime indices run\nfrom $0$ to $3$; spatial indices run from $1$ to $3$ and are contracted with\nthe Euclidean spatial metric. The coordinates $x^a$ have unit metre and\n$\\partial_a:=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$.\n\nThe positive constants $c$, $h$ and $e_{\\rm SI}$ have the exact SI defining\nvalues; write $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. The magnetic constant $\\mu_0>0$ has SI unit\n$\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ and is experimentally determined under the current SI;\nno exact numerical value or current estimate for it is adopted. Put\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ when that symbol is used. These are SI\nnormalization inputs, not empirical claims about a selected QFT model.\n\nFor a species with rest mass $m_{\\rm rest,SI}>0$ in kilograms define its\ninverse-length mass parameter\n\n$$\\mu:=\\frac{m_{\\rm rest,SI}c}{\\hbar}\\quad[\\mathrm m^{-1}].$$\n\nFor a spatial wave vector $\\mathbf k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, with each component in\n$\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and the Euclidean norm, define\n$\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k):=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}\\in\\mathbb R_{>0}$, with\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Here $\\omega_\\mu$ is the temporal wave number, not the\nSI angular frequency; the corresponding SI angular frequency is\n$c\\omega_\\mu$. Energy and momentum are $E=\\hbar c\\omega_\\mu$ in joules and\n$\\mathbf p=\\hbar\\mathbf k$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$. With\n$p=(\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k),\\mathbf k)\\in\\mathbb R^4$ and\n$x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)\\in\\mathbb R^4$, the phase convention is\n\n$$p\\mathbin{\\circ}x:=\\omega_\\mu(\\mathbf k)x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\n\nFor a selected local action represented on a chart region\n$\\mathcal U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ by\n$$\\frac{S}{\\hbar}=\\int_{\\mathcal U}\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}(x)\\,d^4x,\\quad d^4x:=dx^0dx^1dx^2dx^3,$$\nthe natural Lagrangian density $\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ has unit\n$\\mathrm m^{-4}$. Since $x^0=ct$, the corresponding SI Lagrangian density is\n$\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{SI}}:=\\hbar c\\,\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{nat}}$ and has unit\n$\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$. This unit conversion alone does not identify a\nLagrangian density with the model's Hamiltonian energy density.\n\nFor canonical engineering dimensions in this four-dimensional normalization,\nuse the formal field symbols $\\phi_{\\rm n}$ and $\\psi_{\\rm n}$ in the selected\nscalar and Dirac kinetic terms, assigning them length degrees $-1$ and\n$-3/2$, respectively. These are the dimensions fixed by those free kinetic\nterms, not exact scaling dimensions of interacting fields: anomalous\ndimensions, when present, are separate quantities. These symbols do not\nassert pointwise field maps or operator fields; a model supplies its field\nspace and regularity. Let $A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}=(\\varphi/c,\\mathbf A)$ and\n$F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}$ use the component convention of\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], namely\n$F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, with\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$ only when a potential is\nsupplied. Define the normalized photon potential and field strength by\n\n$$a^\\mu:=\\frac{A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}},\\qquad f^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}}.$$\n\nThey have length degrees $-1$ and $-2$, respectively. The EM supplier is used\nonly for SI units and tensor components: no classical Maxwell equation,\nLorentz-force law, or classical-to-quantum identification is adopted here.\n\nFor any separately declared SI charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}$ in coulombs put\n\n$$q_{\\rm n}:=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}.$$\n\nIt is dimensionless. For the positive exact SI elementary charge use\n$e_{\\rm n}:=e_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$ and define the rationalized\ndimensionless coupling $\\alpha:=e_{\\rm n}^2/(4\\pi)$. Since $\\mu_0$ is\nmeasured, these formulas do not assign an exact numerical value to\n$e_{\\rm n}$ or $\\alpha$. If the model uses minimal coupling, its convention is\n$D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu-iq_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$; for an electron with\n$q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$ this reads $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ie_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$.\nBare, renormalized-at-scale, and measured effective charges are separately\nlabelled model parameters; applying the conversion to one label does not\nidentify it with either of the others or specify a running law.\n\nIn a supplied four-component spinor representation, let\n$\\gamma^a\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(\\mathbb C^4)$, $a=0,1,2,3$, be\ndimensionless linear maps satisfying\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}:=\\gamma^a\\gamma^b+\\gamma^b\\gamma^a\n=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$, where $I_4$ is the identity map. Set $\\beta:=\\gamma^0$\nand $\\alpha^i:=\\beta\\gamma^i$; then $\\beta^2=I_4$ and\n$\\gamma^i=\\beta\\alpha^i$. For a supplied four-component spinor $\\psi$ write\n$\\bar\\psi:=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$. The effective Clifford metric is $-\\eta$.\nA supplied free Dirac model may use\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0$ for its model-supplied spinor field\n$\\psi$; that model supplies its field domain and regularity. The phase and\nClifford choices are conventions; this item proves no representation or field\nequation.\n\nIf a selected model supplies a complex Hilbert space $\\mathscr H$ as in\n[[def-hilbert-space]], a complex-linear Hamiltonian operator\n$H:D(H)\\to\\mathscr H$ with model-specified linear domain $D(H)\\subseteq\\mathscr H$\nand SI energy unit joules, and an evolution family $U(t):\\mathscr H\\to\\mathscr H$\nfor $t\\in I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ in seconds, the convention is\n$U(t)=\\exp(-itH/\\hbar)$. Each $U(t)$ is a surjective complex-linear map\nsatisfying $\\langle U(t)\\xi,U(t)\\zeta\\rangle=\\langle\\xi,\\zeta\\rangle$\nfor all $\\xi,\\zeta\\in\\mathscr H$; write $U(t)^*:=U(t)^{-1}$. The exponent\nis dimensionless because $\\hbar$ has unit $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. For any\nmodel-supplied complex-linear operator $O:D(O)\\to\\mathscr H$, with specified\nlinear domain $D(O)\\subseteq\\mathscr H$, define its Heisenberg conjugate by\n$$O_H(t):=U(t)^*O\\,U(t),\\quad\nD(O_H(t)):=\\{\\xi\\in\\mathscr H:U(t)\\xi\\in D(O)\\}.$$\nThe model supplies these domains and the evolution; this dictionary constructs\nno Hamiltonian, evolution family, or field representation. Where a free-field\nmodule uses an inner product linear in its second variable, convert to the\nrepository's first-variable-linear pairing by\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_{\\rm FF}=(v,u)_{\\rm root}$.\n\nFor precision about domains, let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space with\ninner product linear in its first variable and let\n$\\mathscr D\\subseteq\\mathcal H$ be a dense complex linear subspace. A **weak operator-valued distribution\non the common domain $\\mathscr D$** means a complex-linear assignment\n$g\\mapsto\\Phi(g)$ for $g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$, where each\n$\\Phi(g):\\mathscr D\\to\\mathcal H$ is linear and, for every\n$u,v\\in\\mathscr D$, the scalar map\n$g\\mapsto\\langle\\Phi(g)u,v\\rangle$ is a continuous complex-linear functional\non the test-function space with the topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]], hence a distribution as in [[def-distribution]].\nThis definition gives no pointwise field $\\Phi(x)$ and no product\n$\\Phi(x)\\Phi(x)$; a product or extension needs a separate construction. It\nasserts only matrix-element continuity and the stated linear maps; all further\noperator properties, locality, local observable algebras, graded locality,\nand gauge-specific potential behavior must be supplied by a constructed\nmodel. Test-function units can be chosen so a specified smeared observable is\ndimensionless; there is no universal test-function unit asserted here.\n\nPrimitive data for a selected model include its species and rest masses,\ndeclared charge parameters, regulator geometry, couplings, state and any\nconstructed measurement data. Vacuum-subtracted Hamiltonians, regulator\neigenvalues, distributions, correlations and outcome probabilities are\nderived only when their constructions supply the required domains and\nwell-definedness. This dictionary neither constructs an interacting\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space nor asserts pointwise field products,\nscattering, an LSZ limit, asymptotic completeness, or removal of a regulator.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-null-quotient",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and use $\\mathcal H_T$ from [[def-qft-photon-transverse-space]]. On $\\mathcal K=L^2(\\mathbb R^3,d^3\\mathbf k;\\mathbb C^4)$ define\n$$[\\epsilon,\\delta]_\\eta=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\epsilon(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger\\eta\\,\\delta(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k,\\qquad \\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1).$$\nFor $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, let $n=\\mathbf k/|\\mathbf k|$ and set\n$$\\mathcal K_0=\\{\\epsilon\\in\\mathcal K:-\\epsilon^0+n\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\epsilon=0\\text{ a.e.}\\},\\qquad T\\epsilon=\\boldsymbol\\epsilon-n\\epsilon^0.$$\nThen $\\mathcal K_0$ is closed, $T:\\mathcal K_0\\to\\mathcal H_T$ is bounded and onto, and\n$$[\\epsilon,\\delta]_\\eta=\\langle T\\epsilon,T\\delta\\rangle_2\\quad(\\epsilon,\\delta\\in\\mathcal K_0).$$\nIts radical (the vectors pairing to zero with all of $\\mathcal K_0$) is\n$$\\mathcal N=\\ker T=\\{b(\\mathbf k)(1,n(\\mathbf k)):b\\in L^2(\\mathbb R^3)\\}.$$\nConsequently $\\mathcal K_0/\\mathcal N$ with the induced form is a positive Hilbert space isometric to $\\mathcal H_T$. The form on the four-component auxiliary space is indefinite; only the subsidiary quotient is the positive physical one-particle space.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "lem-qft-photon-covariance-spectrum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. On the future null cone write $p=(\\omega,\\mathbf k)$, $\\omega=|\\mathbf k|$, $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, and use the measure $d\\nu=d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\omega)$. The fiber $p^\\perp/\\mathbb Cp$ has the positive quotient form of [[lem-qft-photon-null-quotient]]. Give the proper orthochronous Lorentz group its matrix topology: the subspace topology inherited from $\\mathbb R^{16}$ with Euclidean matrix norm $\\|A\\|_{\\rm mat}=(\\sum_{i,j=0}^3|A_{ij}|^2)^{1/2}$. For each proper orthochronous Lorentz transformation $\\Lambda$, the action\n$$[s(p)]\\longmapsto[\\Lambda s(\\Lambda^{-1}p)]$$\nis a unitary representation on the square-integrable quotient sections. It is strongly continuous for this topology, meaning $\\|U_\\Lambda s-U_{\\Lambda_0}s\\|\\to0$ for every section $s$ as $\\|\\Lambda-\\Lambda_0\\|_{\\rm mat}\\to0$. Its bosonic Fock lift is strongly continuous for the same topology. Under the unitary identification with $\\mathcal H_T$ given by\n$T_p[\\epsilon]:=\\boldsymbol\\epsilon-(\\mathbf k/\\omega)\\epsilon^0$\nand $u(\\mathbf k)=T_p[s(p)]/\\sqrt{2\\omega}$, as in [[lem-qft-photon-null-quotient]], the free translation representation for $a=(a^0,\\mathbf a)\\in\\mathbb R^4$ has phase $e^{i(\\omega a^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf a)}$. Its bosonic Fock lift has the joint PVM $\\mathsf E$ whose $n$-particle sector acts by multiplication by $\\mathbf1_{\\{(\\sum_{r=1}^n|\\mathbf k_r|,-\\sum_{r=1}^n\\mathbf k_r)\\in B\\}}$ for Borel $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$; on the vacuum it acts by $\\mathbf1_B(0)$. Define its joint spectrum to be\n$$\\operatorname{supp}(\\mathsf E):=\\{q\\in\\mathbb R^4:\\mathsf E(U)\\ne0\\text{ for every open neighborhood }U\\text{ of }q\\}.$$\nThen this joint spectrum is the closed future cone\n$$\\overline V_+=\\{(E,\\mathbf P):E\\ge|\\mathbf P|\\},$$\nwith the vacuum at $(0,\\mathbf0)$. In the product realization of the sectors in [[lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum]], on sector $n$ the energy operator multiplies by $\\sum_{r=1}^n|\\mathbf k_r|$ and has the exact maximal domain\n$$D(H_E)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\int_{(\\mathbb R^3)^n}\\left(\\sum_{r=1}^n|\\mathbf k_r|\\right)^2\\|\\Psi_n(\\mathbf k_1,\\ldots,\\mathbf k_n)\\|^2\\,d^3\\mathbf k_1\\cdots d^3\\mathbf k_n<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nwith the vacuum component unrestricted; here $\\Psi_n$ is the symmetric transverse wavefunction in the product realization and the norm is the product-fiber Hilbert norm. Each momentum operator $P_j$ multiplies by $-\\sum_{r=1}^n k_{r,j}$ and has maximal domain\n$$D(P_j)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\int_{(\\mathbb R^3)^n}\\left|\\sum_{r=1}^n k_{r,j}\\right|^2\\|\\Psi_n(\\mathbf k_1,\\ldots,\\mathbf k_n)\\|^2\\,d^3\\mathbf k_1\\cdots d^3\\mathbf k_n<\\infty\\right\\},$$\nagain with unrestricted vacuum component. These are mathematical free-field representation statements.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]]. Let\n$(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a sigma-finite measure space and let\n$\\boldsymbol\\omega=(\\omega_1,\\ldots,\\omega_d):X\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be a\nfinite-valued measurable map, where $d\\ge1$. Put\n$H=L^2(X,\\mu;\\mathbb C)$ with the second-variable-linear pairing of\n[[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]], and form the bosonic Fock space\n$\\mathcal F_s(H)$. For $n\\ge1$, identify $H_s^{(n)}$ with the symmetric\nsubspace of $L^2(X^n,\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}};\\mathbb C)$ by iterating\n[[lem-qft-l2-product-tensor-identification]]. Set $X^0=\\{\\star\\}$,\n$s_0(\\star)=0$, and for $n\\ge1$ set\n$$s_n(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\sum_{r=1}^n\\boldsymbol\\omega(x_r)\\in\\mathbb R^d.$$\nFor $1\\le j\\le d$, define $H_j$ by multiplication by the $j$th coordinate\n$s_{n,j}$ on sector $n$, with exact domain\n$$D(H_j)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n): \\sum_{n\\ge1}\\int_{X^n}|s_{n,j}|^2|\\Psi_n|^2\\,d\\overline{\\mu^{\\times n}}<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nEach $H_j$ is self-adjoint, and $\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}\\cap D(H_j)$ is a\ngraph core. The joint projection-valued measure\n[[def-projection-valued-measure]] on $\\mathbb R^d$ is\n$$(E(B)\\Psi)_n=\\mathbf1_{s_n^{-1}(B)}\\Psi_n\\quad(n\\ge1),\\qquad (E(B)\\Psi)_0=\\mathbf1_B(0)\\Psi_0.$$\nFor $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^d$, let $H_\\theta$ be multiplication on sector $n$\nby $\\theta\\cdot s_n$, with its maximal square-integrability domain. Then\n$$U_\\theta(t)\\Psi:=\\bigl(e^{it\\theta\\cdot s_n}\\Psi_n\\bigr)_{n\\ge0}$$\nis a strongly continuous unitary group on all of Fock space, and its norm\nderivative at zero exists exactly for $\\Psi\\in D(H_\\theta)$, where it is\n$iH_\\theta\\Psi$. The zero-particle sector is the vacuum eigenvector at joint\nvalue $0$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $\\mathbf k\\ne0$, $|\\mathbf n|^2=1$, so\n$P_T^*=P_T$ and\n$P_T^2=(I-\\mathbf n\\mathbf n^{\\mathsf T})^2\n=I-2\\mathbf n\\mathbf n^{\\mathsf T}\n+\\mathbf n(\\mathbf n^{\\mathsf T}\\mathbf n)\\mathbf n^{\\mathsf T}\n=P_T$. Its kernel is $\\operatorname{span}\\{\\mathbf n\\}$, so its range is\nthe two-dimensional plane $\\mathbf n^\\perp$. As a measurable bounded\northogonal projection, its multiplication operator is idempotent on\n$L^2$. If $P_Tu_r\\to u$ with $P_Tu_r=u_r$, boundedness gives\n$P_Tu=\\lim_rP_Tu_r=\\lim_ru_r=u$; hence the range is closed. A change at\n$\\mathbf k=0$ changes no $L^2$ vector because that singleton has Lebesgue\nmeasure zero. This gives a positive rank-two transverse fiber almost\neverywhere without selecting a polarization frame.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "By [L1], each $\\epsilon\\in\\mathcal K_0$ maps to a transverse vector\n$T\\epsilon=\\boldsymbol\\epsilon-\\mathbf n\\epsilon^0$, and\n$[\\epsilon,\\epsilon]_\\eta=\\|T\\epsilon\\|_{\\mathcal H_T}^2\\ge0$.\nThe kernel is exactly $\\mathcal N$, so the induced norm is positive on\nnonzero quotient classes. The supplier proves that $T$ is onto and that the\nquotient is isometric to the complete Hilbert space $\\mathcal H_T$; therefore\nno negative-norm auxiliary direction becomes a physical state in this\nquotient. This conclusion is conditional on the current draft interface.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The free Fock construction is understood under Countable Choice [A2].\nBy [F3], the unscaled multiplier $d\\Gamma(|\\mathbf k|)$ is\nself-adjoint on its exact maximal sector-sum domain. The physical energy\noperator in the selected model is $H_\\gamma=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma(|\\mathbf k|)$,\nso it has the same domain. In sector $n$ it is multiplication by\n$H_n=\\hbar c\\sum_{r=1}^n|\\mathbf k_r|$. Every summand is nonnegative and\n$\\hbar c>0$ by [F2], so $H_n\\ge0$; on the vacuum sector the empty sum is\nzero. The strongly continuous unitary Lorentz action follows from [L2].\nThese are properties of the selected free model and do not assert an\ninteracting theory.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "L2",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
