    "pthm-qft-qed-coulomb-constraints": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "post-qft-qed-local-coupling",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt a smooth local classical Maxwell–Dirac model on an open region of the\nflat spacetime specified by [[def-qft-framework-units]]. Let $A_{{\\rm SI},a}$\nbe a supplied real $C^\\infty$ electromagnetic covector potential and let\n$\\psi:\\!U\\to\\mathbb C^4$ be a $C^\\infty$ commuting spinor field. The charge\n$q_{\\rm SI}$ and rest mass $m_{\\rm SI}>0$ are model inputs; for the electron\ntake $q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$. Use the component, metric, Clifford, charge,\nfield-normalization, and unit conventions of\n[[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]], [[def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities]],\n[[def-qft-framework-units]], and the electromagnetic suppliers of\n[[post-qft-free-photon-model]]. In particular,\n$D_a=\\partial_a-iq_{\\rm SI}A_{{\\rm SI},a}/\\hbar\n=\\partial_a-iq_{\\rm n}a_a$ and $\\mu=m_{\\rm SI}c/\\hbar$.\n\nAdopt the local action density in natural normalization\n\n$$\\mathcal L_{\\rm nat} =-\\frac14 f_{ab}f^{ab} +\\frac{i}{2}\\bigl(\\bar\\psi\\gamma^aD_a\\psi -(D_a\\bar\\psi)\\gamma^a\\psi\\bigr)-\\mu\\bar\\psi\\psi,$$\n\nwhere $f_{ab}=\\partial_a a_b-\\partial_ba_a$ on the region where the\npotential is supplied, $\\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$, and\n$D_a\\bar\\psi=\\partial_a\\bar\\psi+iq_{\\rm n}a_a\\bar\\psi$. The physical action\nis $S_{\\rm cl}=\\hbar\\int_U\\mathcal L_{\\rm nat}\\,d^4x$ with $x^0=ct$. For\ncompactly supported local variations this is the adopted classical\nMaxwell–Dirac formulation; its Euler–Lagrange consequences are not postulated\nas a proof in this item.\n\nFor a smooth gauge function $\\chi$ in webers, the adopted local\ntransformation is\n$A_{{\\rm SI},a}\\mapsto A_{{\\rm SI},a}+\\partial_a\\chi$ and\n$\\psi\\mapsto e^{iq_{\\rm SI}\\chi/\\hbar}\\psi$. It leaves $F=dA$ invariant and\nmakes $D_a\\psi$ transform by the same phase. This is the covector form of the\nSI potential rule in [[def-em-gauge-transformation]]. Gauge functions in the\nlocal variation argument are compactly supported; no assertion is made about\nlarge gauge transformations, nontrivial bundles, boundary charges, or\nasymptotic sectors.\n\nThe model adopts the separately specified free Dirac representation of\n[[post-qft-free-field-model]] and the positive free photon representation of\n[[post-qft-free-photon-model]] as the free-field correspondence used to\ndescribe the selected species. This extra correspondence does not quantize\nthe interacting classical action. In particular, this postulate supplies no\ninteracting continuum four-dimensional QED Hilbert space, no globally\nwell-posed coupled PDE, no pointwise operator-valued composite current, no\nscattering or LSZ construction, and no regulator-removal or\nnonperturbative-existence result.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the oriented, time-oriented Minkowski model, length coordinates, metric,\nand SI/natural dictionary of [[def-qft-framework-units]], with\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and $x^0=ct$. Use the gamma matrices,\nDirac adjoint, and effective Clifford metric of\n[[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]: $\\gamma^0=\\beta$, $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$,\nand $\\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta$. Assume Countable Choice\n[[def-countable-choice]] for the mathematical QFT gauge-data supplier; the\nunit conversions below do not themselves spend a choice principle.\n\nIdentify the SI charge and mass symbols $q,m$ in\n[[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]] with $q_{\\rm SI},m_{\\rm SI}$ here. Its natural\ncoupling $g$ is $q_{\\rm n}$ below, and its inverse-length mass $\\mu$ is the\nsame $m_{\\rm SI}c/\\hbar$.\n\nLet $q_{\\rm SI}\\in\\mathbb R$ be a selected charge parameter in coulombs and\n$m_{\\rm SI}>0$ a selected rest-mass parameter in kilograms. They are inputs\nof the chosen model; neither is derived from Maxwell's equations. Set\n$\\mu=m_{\\rm SI}c/\\hbar$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and\n$q_{\\rm n}=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$, which is dimensionless under the\nframework units of [[def-qft-framework-units]]. For an electron use\n$q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$ and\n$e_{\\rm n}=e_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}>0$, so $q_{\\rm n}=-e_{\\rm n}$.\nUse $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ with the measured current-SI\n$\\mu_0$ of [[def-qft-framework-units]]. These definitions therefore do not\nassign an exact numerical value to $q_{\\rm n}$, $e_{\\rm n}$, or\n$\\varepsilon_0$.\n\nIn a named inertial chart, let $A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}=(\\varphi/c,\\mathbf A)$ be the\nelectromagnetic potential when one is supplied, with components in\n$\\mathrm{T\\,m}$ under [[def-em-si-conventions]] and\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]]. Define the canonical potential and\nfield-strength components by\n\n$$a^\\mu:=\\frac{A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}},\\qquad f^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}}.$$\n\nThey have engineering dimensions $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ and $\\mathrm m^{-2}$,\nrespectively. With the metric-lowered potential\n$A_{{\\rm SI},a}=\\eta_{ab}A^b_{\\rm SI}$, the potential component convention is\n$A_{{\\rm SI},0}=-\\varphi/c$ and $A_{{\\rm SI},i}=A_i$. The covariant derivative\nin the selected QED model is\n\n$$D_a:=\\partial_a-iq_{\\rm n}a_a =\\partial_a-\\frac{iq_{\\rm SI}}{\\hbar}A_{{\\rm SI},a},$$\n\nsince $q_{\\rm n}a_a=q_{\\rm SI}A_{{\\rm SI},a}/\\hbar$. Both terms in $D_a$\nhave unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. If the SI gauge function $\\chi$ has unit\n$\\mathrm{Wb}=\\mathrm{T\\,m^2}$, then $q_{\\rm SI}\\chi/\\hbar$ is dimensionless;\nthe corresponding natural gauge function\n$\\lambda=\\chi/\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}$ is dimensionless and\n$q_{\\rm n}\\lambda=q_{\\rm SI}\\chi/\\hbar$. These conversions apply to smooth\nlocal gauge transformations; they assert no boundary condition, asymptotic\ncharge sector, or global gauge-fixing result.\n\nIf a separately specified finite regulator introduces Hamiltonian\neigenvalues, they have unit joule; this definition introduces no regulator.\nThe field components and units refer to the selected inertial chart, not to a\nnew observer congruence or a cross-framework identification.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-em-electromagnetic-tensor",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let the laboratory region, its inertial chart and the fields and sources be those\nof [[def-em-lab-geometry]], [[def-em-fields]] and [[def-em-sources]], with the\nunits of [[def-em-si-conventions]]; fix the ordered basis\n$(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$ of the chart and the oriented volume form $\\varepsilon$ of\n[[def-oriented-minkowski-volume-form]], with $\\varepsilon_{0123}=+1$ and\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$.\n\n(a) [Components] For a field pair $(E,B)$ of class $C^k$, $k\\ge1$, define the\ncovariant components of the **electromagnetic two-form** $F$ in the chart by\n$F_{00}=0$, $F_{0i}:=-E_i/c$, $F_{ij}:=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$\n($i,j,k\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$) and $F_{ab}:=-F_{ba}$, the index-raised components being\n$F^{0i}=E_i/c$, $F^{i0}=-E_i/c$ and $F^{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$. Here\n$\\epsilon_{ijk}$ is the spatial permutation symbol of\n[[def-oriented-minkowski-volume-form]]. Then $F$ is a $C^k$ type-$(0,2)$\ntensor field on $U$ in the sense of\n[[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]], alternating at every event, and the\ncomponents above are exactly its component functions: at $p\\in U$ the tensor\n$F(p)$ is the bilinear form $F(p)(u,w)=F_{ab}(p)u^aw^b$ on $V$ in the\ncoordinates $u^a,w^b$ of $u,w$ in $(e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3)$. Indeed each $F_{ab}$ is\na finite sum of products of the $C^k$ functions $E_i,B_k$ of the chart\ncoordinates with the constants $c,\\epsilon_{ijk}$, hence a $C^k$ function of\n$x(p)$, and the component criterion of\n[[def-ck-tensor-field-on-minkowski-space]] applies.\n\n(b) [Observer decomposition] Let $n:=e_0$ be the future unit timelike vector of\nthe chart and let $e_a:=F_{ab}n^b$, $b_a:=(*F)_{ab}n^b$ be the observer\ncomponents of [[thm-sr-antisymmetric-observer-decomposition]]; then\n$e_i=+E_i/c$, $b_i=-B_i$ and $e_0=b_0=0$, and the invariants and square wedge\nof that theorem read\n\n$$\\tfrac12F_{ab}F^{ab}=|B|^2-|E|^2/c^2,\\qquad F_{ab}(*F)^{ab}=+4\\,(E\\cdot B)/c,\\qquad F\\wedge F=-2\\,(E\\cdot B)\\,\\varepsilon/c,$$\n\nwhere $|\\cdot|$ and $\\cdot$ are the spatial Euclidean structure of the frame.\n(For the same pair $(E,B)$, that theorem's optional clause (d) uses\n$F_{\\rm optional}^{i0}=E_i/c$ and $F_{\\rm optional}^{ij}=-\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, so\n$F_{\\rm optional}=-F$ in all six components, not only the magnetic block. This\nframework fixes clause (a) and uses the theorem's general algebra; its optional\nSI dictionary must be converted by negating the entire tensor.)\n\n(c) [Potentials when they exist] If $\\varphi$ and $A$ are $C^1$ with\n$E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$ and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$, and\n$A^\\mu:=(\\varphi/c,A)$, $A_\\mu:=\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}A^\\nu$, then\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$; conversely, if a $C^1$\ncovector field $A_\\mu$ on $U$ satisfies $F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu\n-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$, then $E,B$ recovered from $F$ by clause (a) obey\n$E=-\\nabla\\varphi-\\partial_tA$ and $B=\\operatorname{curl}A$ with\n$\\varphi:=-cA_0$ and the spatial potential vector $A:=(A_1,A_2,A_3)$. Here\n$\\nabla$ and $\\operatorname{curl}$ are those of\n[[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] in the spatial coordinates,\nand the spatial cross-product conventions of [[def-cross-product-in-r3]] are\nused. The definition does not assert that every field pair admits such a\npotential; where a $C^1$ potential represents the $C^1$-or-better field of\nclause (a), the field is closed in the explicit sense\n$\\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu}+\\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda}\n+\\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu}=0$. This conclusion does not require second\nclassical derivatives of the potential: put\n$H_{\\lambda\\mu\\nu}:=\\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu}+\\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda}\n+\\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu}$, which is continuous because $F$ is $C^1$; for\nevery smooth compactly supported test $\\psi$, substituting $F=dA$ and\nintegrating by parts twice, moving the derivatives first onto $\\psi$\n([[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]] (b)) and then off the\npotential factors after commuting only the smooth test derivatives\n([[thm-clairaut-schwarz-mixed-partials]]), gives $\\int H\\psi=0$; the\nfundamental lemma (a) of [[lem-sr-compact-variation-and-fundamental-lemma]]\nthen gives $H=0$. The framework's potential theorems establish existence only\nunder their stated hypotheses; [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]]\nsupplies the coordinate derivatives $\\partial_\\mu$ used throughout.\n\n(d) [Four-current and index-raising operator] With the adopted four-current\n$j=(c\\rho,J)$ of [[def-em-sources]], the raised-index operator $\\Phi$ of\n[[lem-em-antisymmetric-tensor-contraction]], defined pointwise on $V$ by\n$\\eta(\\Phi(u),w)=F(w,u)$, satisfies\n$\\Phi(u)^\\mu=\\eta^{\\mu\\alpha}F_{\\alpha\\beta}u^\\beta$; the components of $F$\ncarry the tesla (T), those of $j$ carry A m$^{-2}$, and those of a\nfour-potential $A^\\mu$ carry T m.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "post-em-vacuum-maxwell",
          "source_section": "Postulate",
          "quote": "Adopt as a physical postulate of this framework the following four equations for\nthe fields of [[def-em-fields]] and the sources of [[def-em-sources]] on the\nlaboratory region $U\\cong I\\times\\Omega$ of [[def-em-lab-geometry]], with\n$\\mu_0>0$, $c>0$, $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ and the SI units of\n[[def-em-si-conventions]].\n\n(a) [Smooth branch] Let $E,B$ be jointly $C^1$ fields with the additional\nderivatives required by each equation existing and continuous, and let\n$\\rho,J$ be continuous. Then, with the divergence and curl of\n[[def-divergence-and-curl-of-a-c1-vector-field]] and the partial derivative\n$\\partial_t$ at fixed position of\n[[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]],\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}B=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}E=-\\partial_tB,\\qquad \\operatorname{curl}B=\\mu_0J+c^{-2}\\partial_tE.$$\n\n(b) [Weak branch] Let instead $E,B$ be locally integrable $\\mathbb R^3$-valued\nfields and $\\rho,J$ locally integrable scalar and vector fields on\n$I\\times\\Omega$, identified with their regular distributions\n([[def-regular-distribution-from-a-locally-integrable-function]],\n[[thm-locally-integrable-functions-embed-in-distributions]]). Let\n$\\operatorname{div}$, $\\operatorname{curl}$ and $\\partial_t$ act as\ndistributional derivatives, componentwise, and let $\\psi$ range over\n$C_c^\\infty(I\\times\\Omega)$ ([[def-test-function-space-d-of-an-open-set]]).\nThen the same four identities hold in the sense of [[def-distribution]]: the\npairings\n\n$$\\langle\\operatorname{div}E-\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\psi\\rangle,\\qquad \\langle\\operatorname{div}B,\\psi\\rangle,\\qquad \\langle\\operatorname{curl}E+\\partial_tB,\\psi\\rangle,\\qquad \\langle\\operatorname{curl}B-\\mu_0J-c^{-2}\\partial_tE,\\psi\\rangle$$\n\nall vanish for every such $\\psi$, all derivatives being distributional\nderivatives ([[def-distributional-derivative]]).\n\n(c) [Scope] The postulate contains no magnetic charge: $\\operatorname{div}B=0$\nand no magnetic-source term appears in $\\operatorname{curl}E$. Sources that\nare not locally integrable functions (point or sheet sources) are covered by\nneither branch and require their own definition. The equations are adopted\nclassical vacuum physics in the named inertial chart; they are not deduced from\ncharge conservation, from empirical agreement or from the special-relativity\npostulates, and this item by itself asserts no covariance under changes of\ninertial frame.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "pthm-qft-qed-classical-coupling",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]] for the selected free-field\ncorrespondence in [[post-qft-qed-local-coupling]]. Let $U$ be an open subset\nof flat Minkowski spacetime with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and\nlength coordinates $x^0=ct$. Let $A_{{\\rm SI},a}:U\\to\\mathbb R$ be a supplied\n$C^\\infty$ covector potential, let\n$\\psi:U\\to\\mathbb C^4$ be a $C^\\infty$ commuting spinor, and take a selected\nreal charge $q_{\\rm SI}$ and mass $m_{\\rm SI}>0$ with the conventions of\n[[def-qft-qed-physical-gauge-quantities]] and\n[[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. Use independent compactly supported smooth\nvariations of $A_{\\rm SI}$ and of both real and imaginary components of $\\psi$. If $(A_{\\rm SI},\\psi)$ is stationary\nfor the adopted classical action, then\n\n$$\\bigl(i\\gamma^aD_a-\\mu\\bigr)\\psi=0,\\qquad \\partial_aF_{\\rm SI}^{ab}=-\\mu_0j^b,\\qquad \\partial_aj^a=0,$$\n\nwhere $D_a=\\partial_a-iq_{\\rm SI}A_{{\\rm SI},a}/\\hbar$,\n$\\mu=m_{\\rm SI}c/\\hbar$, and\n$j^a=q_{\\rm SI}c\\,\\bar\\psi\\gamma^a\\psi$. In laboratory components this\ncurrent is $\\rho_q=q_{\\rm SI}\\psi^\\dagger\\psi$ and\n$\\mathbf J_q=q_{\\rm SI}c\\,\\psi^\\dagger\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\psi$, so\n$j=(c\\rho_q,\\mathbf J_q)$. With $[\\psi]=\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$,\n$[q_{\\rm SI}]=\\mathrm C$, and $[c]=\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, the charge density\n$\\rho_q$ has unit $\\mathrm{C\\,m^{-3}}$, while $\\mathbf J_q$ and every\ncomponent of $j^a$ have unit $\\mathrm{A\\,m^{-2}}$; here $x^0=ct$, so\n$j^0=c\\rho_q$ has the same component unit as $J_q^i$. Together with $F=dA$\non the supplied potential domain, the field equation and Bianchi identity\nhave exactly the smooth SI\nMaxwell signs in [[post-em-vacuum-maxwell]]. The current and spinor here are\nclassical smooth commuting fields; this statement is not a construction of a\npointwise quantum composite operator.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-qft-dirac-projections",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], the mathematical scalar shell conventions of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]], and the second-variable-linear Hilbert pairing of [[def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces]]. In the fixed inertial coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)$ of [[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]] on the affine Minkowski model [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]], $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$; these coordinates have length units under [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. The parameter $\\mu\\ge0$ and momentum coordinate $\\mathbf k$ have inverse-length units, and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ has the same units. The parameter is not identified here with a physical rest mass. Matrix $h_D$ has inverse-length units; the dimensionless spin matrices have no physical unit assignment in this mathematical item.\n\nUse the Pauli matrices $\\sigma_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $\\sigma_2=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}$, and $\\sigma_3=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}$. On $\\mathbb C^4=\\mathbb C^2\\oplus\\mathbb C^2$, define\n$$\\beta=\\begin{pmatrix}I_2&0\\\\0&-I_2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\alpha_j=\\begin{pmatrix}0&\\sigma_j\\\\\\sigma_j&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad \\gamma^0=\\beta,\\qquad \\gamma^j=\\beta\\alpha_j.$$\nFor a spinor column $z\\in\\mathbb C^4$, its Dirac adjoint is $\\bar z=z^\\dagger\\beta$. The Clifford contraction is $\\Gamma(p):=\\gamma^0p^0-\\sum_{j=1}^3\\gamma^jp_j$; this contraction uses the effective matrix $-\\eta$ in the relation $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$.\n\nLet $\\mathcal H_D$ be the space of measurable functions $u:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb C^4$, modulo equality almost everywhere, with\n$$\\|u\\|^2=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger u(\\mathbf k)\\,\\frac{d^3\\mathbf k}{(2\\pi)^3}<\\infty,$$\nand pairing $\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\int u(\\mathbf k)^\\dagger v(\\mathbf k)\\,d^3\\mathbf k/(2\\pi)^3$. Define the measurable matrix $h_D(\\mathbf k)=\\sum_j\\alpha_j k_j+\\beta\\mu$ and its maximal multiplication operator with domain $D(h_D)=\\{u\\in\\mathcal H_D:h_Du\\in\\mathcal H_D\\}$. For $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix fields\n$$P_\\pm(\\mathbf k)=\\frac12\\left(I_4\\pm\\frac{h_D(\\mathbf k)}{\\omega(\\mathbf k)}\\right).$$\nWhen $\\mu=0$ at $\\mathbf k=0$, set $P_+(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,0)$ and $P_-(0)=\\operatorname{diag}(0,I_2)$. These are complementary rank-two orthogonal projections; the value at this Lebesgue-null point does not change their multiplication operators. Write $M_{P_\\pm}$ for their maximal multiplication operators and $\\mathcal H_\\pm=\\operatorname{ran}M_{P_\\pm}$. The companion matrix lemma on this page proves these are bounded orthogonal projections with closed ranges. The conjugate Hilbert space $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$ has vectors $\\bar u$, scalar multiplication $z\\bar u=\\overline{\\bar z u}$, and pairing $\\langle\\bar u,\\bar v\\rangle=\\langle v,u\\rangle$.\n\nUse the sine and cosine definitions of [[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]]. For a length-coordinate increment $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $\\omega(\\mathbf k)>0$, define the matrix multiplier\n$$W_D(t,\\mathbf k)=\\cos(\\omega t)I_4+i\\frac{\\sin(\\omega t)}{\\omega}h_D(\\mathbf k).$$\nAt the massless apex set $W_D(t,0)=I_4$. Its unitarity, group law and generator identity are proved by the companion matrix lemma on this page.\n\nThe symbols $P_+$ and $P_-$ designate the positive- and negative-energy matrix formulas for this fixed free Dirac operator. This is purely mathematical data; no physical spin-statistics postulate or empirical assertion is included.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-qft-qed-gauge-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the fixed Minkowski coordinates $x=(x^0,\\mathbf x)=(ct,x^1,x^2,x^3)$, with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, on an open coordinate domain $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$ with its standard topology. Each $x^a$ has unit metre. For a smooth scalar component $u:U\\to\\mathbb R$ or $u:U\\to\\mathbb C$, define $\\partial_a u:=\\partial u/\\partial x^a$; thus $\\partial_0=c^{-1}\\partial_t$ at fixed $\\mathbf x$, $\\partial_j=\\partial/\\partial x^j$ for $j=1,2,3$, and each $\\partial_a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Raise derivative indices with $\\partial^a:=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_b$. The fields in this definition are smooth maps\n$$\\phi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R),\\qquad \\mathbf A\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R^3),\\qquad \\psi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb C^4),\\qquad \\chi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R).$$\nDefine the lower-potential component maps $A_a:U\\to\\mathbb R$ by $A_0=-\\phi/c$ and $A_j=\\mathbf A_j$; collectively $(A_a)$ is the real covector potential in this chart. Then $F_{ab}:=\\partial_aA_b-\\partial_bA_a$ has smooth real component maps $F_{ab}:U\\to\\mathbb R$. The spinor is an ordinary commuting $\\mathbb C^4$-valued map, not a tuple of Grassmann generators. Its Dirac adjoint is the smooth row-valued map $\\bar\\psi:=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta:U\\to(\\mathbb C^4)^*$, using the fixed matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]], so $\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}I_4$. In fixed SI units, $\\phi$ is in volts, each $A_a$ is in $\\mathrm{T\\,m}$, $\\psi$ is assigned field dimension $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$, and $\\chi$ is in webers; these unit labels accompany the maps and do not identify $q$ with a measured charge. For periodic spatial domains, the same component and unit conventions apply to smooth periodic coordinate lifts, with the quotient domain specified by the item using them.\n\nFor the SI-normalization bookkeeping, let $c>0$ (m s$^{-1}$), $\\hbar>0$ (J s), $\\mu_0>0$ (N A$^{-2}$), a real charge parameter $q$ (C), and a mass parameter $m>0$ (kg) be symbolic inputs; put $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$. In the current SI, $c$ and $h$ are defining constants while $\\mu_0$ is experimentally determined; the formula below keeps $\\mu_0$ symbolic and assigns it no numerical value. Use the lower-potential convention\n$$A_a=(-\\phi/c,\\mathbf A),$$\nwhere $A_a$ has units T m, $\\phi$ is in volts, and $\\mathbf A$ is in T m. Define\n$$S_0=\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c},\\qquad a_a=A_a/S_0,\\qquad f_{ab}=\\partial_aa_b-\\partial_ba_a,$$\n$$\\mu=mc/\\hbar,\\qquad g=q\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}=q/\\sqrt{\\epsilon_0\\hbar c},\\qquad D_a=\\partial_a-ig a_a.$$\nHere $D_a$ acts on $\\psi\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb C^4)$ by $(D_a\\psi)(x)=\\partial_a\\psi(x)-ig a_a(x)\\psi(x)$, again a smooth map $U\\to\\mathbb C^4$. Thus $a_a$ and $\\mu$ have units m$^{-1}$, $f_{ab}$ has units m$^{-2}$, and $g$ is dimensionless. For $\\psi$ with units m$^{-3/2}$, both $-\\tfrac14 f_{ab}f^{ab}$ and $\\bar\\psi(i\\gamma^aD_a-\\mu)\\psi$ have units m$^{-4}$; their integral over $d^4x$ is dimensionless and represents the action divided by $\\hbar$ in this normalization.\n\nFor the real smooth gauge-parameter map $\\chi:U\\to\\mathbb R$ in webers, put $\\alpha:=\\chi/S_0\\in C^\\infty(U;\\mathbb R)$ (dimensionless) and use\n$$A'_a=A_a+\\partial_a\\chi,\\qquad a'_a=a_a+\\partial_a\\alpha,\\qquad \\psi'=e^{ig\\alpha}\\psi.$$\nThe induced covariant derivative is $D'_a=\\partial_a-ig a'_a$. These are formal mathematical conventions for the later conditional deductions. They do not adopt a measured value of $\\mu_0$, identify $q$ with an electron charge, impose charge quantization, or assert that interacting continuum fields exist.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-qft-framework-units",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix the oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime and an inertial\nchart of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use signature\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, length coordinates\n$x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres, and coordinate time $t$ in seconds as in\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Greek spacetime indices run\nfrom $0$ to $3$; spatial indices run from $1$ to $3$ and are contracted with\nthe Euclidean spatial metric. The coordinates $x^a$ have unit metre and\n$\\partial_a:=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$.\n\nThe positive constants $c$, $h$ and $e_{\\rm SI}$ have the exact SI defining\nvalues; write $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. The magnetic constant $\\mu_0>0$ has SI unit\n$\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ and is experimentally determined under the current SI;\nno exact numerical value or current estimate for it is adopted. Put\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ when that symbol is used. These are SI\nnormalization inputs, not empirical claims about a selected QFT model.\n\nFor a species with rest mass $m_{\\rm rest,SI}>0$ in kilograms define its\ninverse-length mass parameter\n\n$$\\mu:=\\frac{m_{\\rm rest,SI}c}{\\hbar}\\quad[\\mathrm m^{-1}].$$\n\nFor spatial wave vector $\\mathbf k$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ put\n$\\omega(\\mathbf k):=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Here\n$\\omega$ is the temporal wave number, not the SI angular frequency; the\ncorresponding SI angular frequency is $c\\omega$. Energy and momentum are\n$E=\\hbar c\\omega$ in joules and $\\mathbf p=\\hbar\\mathbf k$ in\n$\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$. With $p=(\\omega,\\mathbf k)$ the phase convention is\n\n$$p\\mathbin{\\circ}x:=\\omega x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\n\nThe natural action is $S/\\hbar$. Its canonical density in four dimensions has\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-4}$; the corresponding physical energy density is\n$\\hbar c$ times that density and has unit $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$.\n\nFor canonical engineering dimensions in this four-dimensional normalization,\nwrite $\\phi_{\\rm n}$ for a scalar field with length degree $-1$ and\n$\\psi_{\\rm n}$ for a Dirac field with length degree $-3/2$. These are the\ndimensions fixed by the selected free kinetic terms, not exact scaling\ndimensions of interacting fields: anomalous dimensions, when present, are\nseparate quantities. Let $A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}=(\\varphi/c,\\mathbf A)$ and\n$F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}$ use the component convention of\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], namely\n$F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, with\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$ only when a potential is\nsupplied. Define the normalized photon potential and field strength by\n\n$$a^\\mu:=\\frac{A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}},\\qquad f^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}}.$$\n\nThey have length degrees $-1$ and $-2$, respectively. The EM supplier is used\nonly for SI units and tensor components: no classical Maxwell equation,\nLorentz-force law, or classical-to-quantum identification is adopted here.\n\nFor any separately declared SI charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}$ in coulombs put\n\n$$q_{\\rm n}:=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}.$$\n\nIt is dimensionless. For the positive exact SI elementary charge use\n$e_{\\rm n}:=e_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$ and define the rationalized\ndimensionless coupling $\\alpha:=e_{\\rm n}^2/(4\\pi)$. Since $\\mu_0$ is\nmeasured, these formulas do not assign an exact numerical value to\n$e_{\\rm n}$ or $\\alpha$. If the model uses minimal coupling, its convention is\n$D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu-iq_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$; for an electron with\n$q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$ this reads $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ie_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$.\nBare, renormalized-at-scale, and measured effective charges are separately\nlabelled model parameters; applying the conversion to one label does not\nidentify it with either of the others or specify a running law.\n\nThe Clifford convention is $\\gamma^0=\\beta$, $\\gamma^i=\\beta\\alpha^i$,\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}$, so the effective Clifford metric is\n$-\\eta$. In a supplied free Dirac model the displayed equation is\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0$. The phase and Clifford choices are\nconventions; this item proves no Dirac representation or field equation.\nTime evolution is written $U(t)=\\exp(-itH/\\hbar)$ when a selected model has\nactually constructed a self-adjoint Hamiltonian $H$ and its unitary group; the\ncorresponding Heisenberg conjugation is $O_H(t)=U(t)^*O\\,U(t)$. No such\nHamiltonian or group is constructed here. Where a free-field module uses an\ninner product linear in its second variable, convert to the repository's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing by\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_{\\rm FF}=(v,u)_{\\rm root}$.\n\nFor precision about domains, let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space with\ninner product linear in its first variable and let\n$\\mathscr D\\subseteq\\mathcal H$ be a dense complex linear subspace. A **weak operator-valued distribution\non the common domain $\\mathscr D$** means a complex-linear assignment\n$g\\mapsto\\Phi(g)$ for $g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$, where each\n$\\Phi(g):\\mathscr D\\to\\mathcal H$ is linear and, for every\n$u,v\\in\\mathscr D$, the scalar map\n$g\\mapsto\\langle\\Phi(g)u,v\\rangle$ is a continuous complex-linear functional\non the test-function space with the topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]], hence a distribution as in [[def-distribution]].\nThis definition gives no pointwise field $\\Phi(x)$ and no product\n$\\Phi(x)\\Phi(x)$; a product or extension needs a separate construction.\nSmeared fields are not thereby bounded, symmetric, self-adjoint, or\nself-adjointly closable. Locality, local observable algebras, graded locality,\nand gauge-specific potential behavior are likewise supplied only by the\nrelevant constructed model modules. Test-function units can be chosen so a\nspecified smeared observable is dimensionless; there is no universal test\nfunction unit asserted here.\n\nPrimitive data for a selected model include its species and rest masses,\ndeclared charge parameters, regulator geometry, couplings, state and any\nconstructed measurement data. Vacuum-subtracted Hamiltonians, regulator\neigenvalues, distributions, correlations and outcome probabilities are\nderived only when their constructions supply the required domains and\nwell-definedness. This dictionary neither constructs an interacting\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space nor asserts pointwise field products,\nscattering, an LSZ limit, asymptotic completeness, or removal of a regulator.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L1",
          "source": "lem-qft-qed-coulomb-constraint",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice and the conventions of [[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]]. Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open time interval, with $t$ measured in seconds and $x^0=ct$, and let $T_L^3=(\\mathbb R/L\\mathbb Z)^3$ be the flat spatial torus with period $L>0$ metres and periodic coordinates $\\mathbf x$. Take jointly smooth periodic maps\n$$\\mathbf A:I\\times T_L^3\\to\\mathbb R^3,\\qquad \\phi:I\\times T_L^3\\to\\mathbb R,\\qquad \\rho:I\\times T_L^3\\to\\mathbb R,\\qquad \\mathbf J:I\\times T_L^3\\to\\mathbb R^3.$$\nWrite $\\dot{\\mathbf A}:=\\partial_t\\mathbf A$ for the time partial derivative at fixed $\\mathbf x$, and $\\mathbf B:=\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A$. All spatial differential operators below are the periodic coordinate derivatives, and each displayed spatial integral and Hamiltonian is evaluated at a fixed $t\\in I$; the time argument is suppressed in the formulas. In SI units, $\\mathbf A$ has unit $\\mathrm{T\\,m}$, $\\phi$ has unit $\\mathrm V$, $\\rho$ has unit $\\mathrm{C\\,m^{-3}}$, and $\\mathbf J$ has unit $\\mathrm{A\\,m^{-2}}$. Thus $\\dot{\\mathbf A}$ and $\\nabla\\phi$ both have unit $\\mathrm{V\\,m^{-1}}=\\mathrm{T\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$, and every term of the formal classical Lagrangian density has unit $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$. Let $c>0$ have unit $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, let $\\mu_0>0$ be the symbolic magnetic constant with unit $\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$, and put $\\epsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ with unit $\\mathrm{F\\,m^{-1}}$; no numerical value for these constants is adopted. Define the formal classical density\n$$\\mathcal L=\\frac{\\epsilon_0}{2}|\\dot{\\mathbf A}+\\nabla\\phi|^2-\\frac1{2\\mu_0}|\\nabla\\times\\mathbf A|^2-\\rho\\phi+\\mathbf J\\cdot\\mathbf A.$$\nThe canonical momenta are $\\boldsymbol\\pi_A=\\epsilon_0(\\dot{\\mathbf A}+\\nabla\\phi)$, with unit $\\mathrm{C\\,m^{-2}}$, and $\\pi_\\phi=0$. Periodic integration by parts gives the fixed-time Hamiltonian\n$$H(t)=\\int_{T_L^3}\\left(\\frac{|\\boldsymbol\\pi_A|^2}{2\\epsilon_0}+\\frac{|\\mathbf B|^2}{2\\mu_0}+\\phi(\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A+\\rho)-\\mathbf J\\cdot\\mathbf A\\right)d^3\\mathbf x.$$\nVariation of the multiplier $\\phi$ imposes $\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A+\\rho=0$. In Coulomb gauge $\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf A=0$, this becomes $-\\Delta\\phi=\\rho/\\epsilon_0$. At each fixed $t$, a smooth periodic solution can exist only if $\\int_{T_L^3}\\rho(\\mathbf x,t)\\,d^3\\mathbf x=0$. If $\\int_{T_L^3}\\rho(\\mathbf x,t)\\,d^3\\mathbf x=0$ for every $t\\in I$, there is a unique jointly smooth periodic solution with zero spatial mean, whose Fourier coefficients are\n$$\\widehat\\phi_0(t)=0,\\qquad \\widehat\\phi_k(t)=\\frac{\\widehat\\rho_k(t)}{\\epsilon_0|2\\pi k/L|^2}\\quad(k\\ne0),\\qquad \\widehat\\rho_k(t)=L^{-3}\\int_{[0,L]^3}\\rho(\\mathbf x,t)e^{-2\\pi i k\\cdot\\mathbf x/L}\\,d^3\\mathbf x.$$\nFor every $t\\in I$ it satisfies\n$$\\frac{\\epsilon_0}{2}\\int_{T_L^3}|\\nabla\\phi|^2\\,d^3\\mathbf x=\\frac12\\int_{T_L^3}\\rho\\phi\\,d^3\\mathbf x=\\frac{L^3}{2\\epsilon_0}\\sum_{k\\ne0}\\frac{|\\widehat\\rho_k(t)|^2}{|2\\pi k/L|^2}\\ge0.$$\nThis is a classical conditional solver for smooth periodic neutral data; it makes no claim about quantum density products or nonperiodic boundary conditions.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "L2",
          "source": "lem-qft-qed-dirac-first-order-constraints",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice, the constants in [[def-qft-qed-gauge-data]], $\\hbar>0$, and a finite integer $N\\ge1$. Let the real configuration space be $Q=\\mathbb R^{2N}$ with coordinates $(x_1,y_1,\\ldots,x_N,y_N)$ and let $T^*Q\\cong\\mathbb R^{4N}$ have corresponding real canonical momentum coordinates $(P^x_1,P^y_1,\\ldots,P^x_N,P^y_N)$. On real smooth functions define the canonical Poisson bracket\n$$\\{F,G\\}=\\sum_{j=1}^N\\left(\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial x_j}\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial P^x_j}-\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial P^x_j}\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial x_j}+\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial y_j}\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial P^y_j}-\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial P^y_j}\\frac{\\partial G}{\\partial y_j}\\right),$$\nand extend it $\\mathbb C$-bilinearly to complex-valued smooth functions on $T^*Q$. Define the complex coordinate notation\n$$z_j=x_j+iy_j,\\qquad \\bar z_j=x_j-iy_j,\\qquad p_j=\\frac{P^x_j-iP^y_j}{2},\\qquad \\bar p_j=\\frac{P^x_j+iP^y_j}{2}.$$\nThese definitions give\n$$\\{z_j,p_k\\}=\\delta_{jk},\\qquad \\{\\bar z_j,\\bar p_k\\}=\\delta_{jk},$$\nwith all other elementary brackets zero; the bracket is extended by bilinearity and the Leibniz rule. The first-order kinetic term $\\frac{i\\hbar}{2}\\sum_j(\\bar z_j\\dot z_j-\\dot{\\bar z}_jz_j)$ gives primary constraints\n$$C_j=p_j-\\frac{i\\hbar}{2}\\bar z_j,\\qquad \\bar C_j=\\bar p_j+\\frac{i\\hbar}{2}z_j.$$\nIn the order $(C_1,\\ldots,C_N,\\bar C_1,\\ldots,\\bar C_N)$ their constraint matrix and inverse are\n$$M=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\hbar I_N\\\\i\\hbar I_N&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad M^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-iI_N/\\hbar\\\\iI_N/\\hbar&0\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nHere, “second class” means that the constraint matrix $M_{AB}=\\{\\chi_A,\\chi_B\\}$ is invertible. Thus these constraints are second class. For a real smooth finite-mode Hamiltonian $H\\in C^\\infty(Q;\\mathbb R)$, viewed as independent of the momenta, define the Wirtinger derivative\n$$\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial\\bar z_j}:=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial x_j}+i\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial y_j}\\right).$$\nThe Dirac bracket\n$$\\{F,G\\}_D=\\{F,G\\}-\\{F,\\chi_A\\}(M^{-1})_{AB}\\{\\chi_B,G\\}, \\quad \\chi=(C_1,\\ldots,C_N,\\bar C_1,\\ldots,\\bar C_N),$$\nsatisfies $\\{z_j,\\bar z_k\\}_D=-i\\delta_{jk}/\\hbar$ and $\\{z_j,z_k\\}_D=\\{\\bar z_j,\\bar z_k\\}_D=0$. For a real smooth finite-mode Hamiltonian $H(z,\\bar z)$ the reduced equation is $i\\hbar\\dot z_j=\\partial H/\\partial\\bar z_j$. These are ordinary commuting coordinates; they do not derive CAR by canonical quantization.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-qft-framework-units",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix the oriented, time-oriented affine Minkowski spacetime and an inertial\nchart of [[def-sr-affine-minkowski-model]] and\n[[def-inertial-frame-and-inertial-chart]]. Use signature\n$\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, length coordinates\n$x^0=ct$ and $x^i$ in metres, and coordinate time $t$ in seconds as in\n[[def-sr-si-units-and-coordinate-conventions]]. Greek spacetime indices run\nfrom $0$ to $3$; spatial indices run from $1$ to $3$ and are contracted with\nthe Euclidean spatial metric. The coordinates $x^a$ have unit metre and\n$\\partial_a:=\\partial/\\partial x^a$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-1}$.\n\nThe positive constants $c$, $h$ and $e_{\\rm SI}$ have the exact SI defining\nvalues; write $\\hbar:=h/(2\\pi)$. The magnetic constant $\\mu_0>0$ has SI unit\n$\\mathrm{N\\,A^{-2}}$ and is experimentally determined under the current SI;\nno exact numerical value or current estimate for it is adopted. Put\n$\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ when that symbol is used. These are SI\nnormalization inputs, not empirical claims about a selected QFT model.\n\nFor a species with rest mass $m_{\\rm rest,SI}>0$ in kilograms define its\ninverse-length mass parameter\n\n$$\\mu:=\\frac{m_{\\rm rest,SI}c}{\\hbar}\\quad[\\mathrm m^{-1}].$$\n\nFor spatial wave vector $\\mathbf k$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$ put\n$\\omega(\\mathbf k):=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf k|^2+\\mu^2}$ in $\\mathrm m^{-1}$. Here\n$\\omega$ is the temporal wave number, not the SI angular frequency; the\ncorresponding SI angular frequency is $c\\omega$. Energy and momentum are\n$E=\\hbar c\\omega$ in joules and $\\mathbf p=\\hbar\\mathbf k$ in\n$\\mathrm{kg\\,m\\,s^{-1}}$. With $p=(\\omega,\\mathbf k)$ the phase convention is\n\n$$p\\mathbin{\\circ}x:=\\omega x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x=-\\eta(p,x).$$\n\nThe natural action is $S/\\hbar$. Its canonical density in four dimensions has\nunit $\\mathrm m^{-4}$; the corresponding physical energy density is\n$\\hbar c$ times that density and has unit $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$.\n\nFor canonical engineering dimensions in this four-dimensional normalization,\nwrite $\\phi_{\\rm n}$ for a scalar field with length degree $-1$ and\n$\\psi_{\\rm n}$ for a Dirac field with length degree $-3/2$. These are the\ndimensions fixed by the selected free kinetic terms, not exact scaling\ndimensions of interacting fields: anomalous dimensions, when present, are\nseparate quantities. Let $A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}=(\\varphi/c,\\mathbf A)$ and\n$F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}$ use the component convention of\n[[def-em-electromagnetic-tensor]], namely\n$F_{0i}=-E_i/c$ and $F_{ij}=\\epsilon_{ijk}B_k$, with\n$F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu$ only when a potential is\nsupplied. Define the normalized photon potential and field strength by\n\n$$a^\\mu:=\\frac{A^\\mu_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}},\\qquad f^{\\mu\\nu}:=\\frac{F^{\\mu\\nu}_{\\rm SI}}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\hbar c}}.$$\n\nThey have length degrees $-1$ and $-2$, respectively. The EM supplier is used\nonly for SI units and tensor components: no classical Maxwell equation,\nLorentz-force law, or classical-to-quantum identification is adopted here.\n\nFor any separately declared SI charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}$ in coulombs put\n\n$$q_{\\rm n}:=q_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu_0c}{\\hbar}}.$$\n\nIt is dimensionless. For the positive exact SI elementary charge use\n$e_{\\rm n}:=e_{\\rm SI}\\sqrt{\\mu_0c/\\hbar}$ and define the rationalized\ndimensionless coupling $\\alpha:=e_{\\rm n}^2/(4\\pi)$. Since $\\mu_0$ is\nmeasured, these formulas do not assign an exact numerical value to\n$e_{\\rm n}$ or $\\alpha$. If the model uses minimal coupling, its convention is\n$D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu-iq_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$; for an electron with\n$q_{\\rm SI}=-e_{\\rm SI}$ this reads $D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ie_{\\rm n}a_\\mu$.\nBare, renormalized-at-scale, and measured effective charges are separately\nlabelled model parameters; applying the conversion to one label does not\nidentify it with either of the others or specify a running law.\n\nThe Clifford convention is $\\gamma^0=\\beta$, $\\gamma^i=\\beta\\alpha^i$,\n$\\{\\gamma^a,\\gamma^b\\}=-2\\eta^{ab}$, so the effective Clifford metric is\n$-\\eta$. In a supplied free Dirac model the displayed equation is\n$(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0$. The phase and Clifford choices are\nconventions; this item proves no Dirac representation or field equation.\nTime evolution is written $U(t)=\\exp(-itH/\\hbar)$ when a selected model has\nactually constructed a self-adjoint Hamiltonian $H$ and its unitary group; the\ncorresponding Heisenberg conjugation is $O_H(t)=U(t)^*O\\,U(t)$. No such\nHamiltonian or group is constructed here. Where a free-field module uses an\ninner product linear in its second variable, convert to the repository's\nfirst-variable-linear pairing by\n$\\langle u,v\\rangle_{\\rm FF}=(v,u)_{\\rm root}$.\n\nFor precision about domains, let $\\mathcal H$ be a complex Hilbert space with\ninner product linear in its first variable and let\n$\\mathscr D\\subseteq\\mathcal H$ be a dense complex linear subspace. A **weak operator-valued distribution\non the common domain $\\mathscr D$** means a complex-linear assignment\n$g\\mapsto\\Phi(g)$ for $g\\in\\mathcal D(\\mathbb R^4)$, where each\n$\\Phi(g):\\mathscr D\\to\\mathcal H$ is linear and, for every\n$u,v\\in\\mathscr D$, the scalar map\n$g\\mapsto\\langle\\Phi(g)u,v\\rangle$ is a continuous complex-linear functional\non the test-function space with the topology of\n[[def-test-function-topology]], hence a distribution as in [[def-distribution]].\nThis definition gives no pointwise field $\\Phi(x)$ and no product\n$\\Phi(x)\\Phi(x)$; a product or extension needs a separate construction.\nSmeared fields are not thereby bounded, symmetric, self-adjoint, or\nself-adjointly closable. Locality, local observable algebras, graded locality,\nand gauge-specific potential behavior are likewise supplied only by the\nrelevant constructed model modules. Test-function units can be chosen so a\nspecified smeared observable is dimensionless; there is no universal test\nfunction unit asserted here.\n\nPrimitive data for a selected model include its species and rest masses,\ndeclared charge parameters, regulator geometry, couplings, state and any\nconstructed measurement data. Vacuum-subtracted Hamiltonians, regulator\neigenvalues, distributions, correlations and outcome probabilities are\nderived only when their constructions supply the required domains and\nwell-definedness. This dictionary neither constructs an interacting\nfour-dimensional QED Hilbert space nor asserts pointwise field products,\nscattering, an LSZ limit, asymptotic completeness, or removal of a regulator.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "In the adopted action, $\\rho_q:=q_{\\rm SI}\\psi^\\dagger\\psi$ is the\ncoefficient of the scalar-potential coupling. For the Dirac part, $x^0=ct$\nand $A_0=-\\varphi/c$ give\n$D_0\\psi=c^{-1}(\\dot\\psi+iq_{\\rm SI}\\varphi\\psi/\\hbar)$ and\n$D_0\\bar\\psi=c^{-1}(\\dot{\\bar\\psi}-iq_{\\rm SI}\\varphi\\bar\\psi/\\hbar)$.\nUsing [F5], the time part of $\\hbar c\\mathcal L_{\\rm nat}$ is the canonical\nterm $i\\hbar(\\psi^\\dagger\\dot\\psi-\\dot\\psi^\\dagger\\psi)/2$ minus\n$q_{\\rm SI}\\varphi\\psi^\\dagger\\psi$. The symmetric spatial derivative,\nintegrated by parts on the periodic torus, is\n$i\\hbar c\\psi^\\dagger\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\nabla\\psi$: the integral\nof $\\nabla\\cdot(\\psi^\\dagger\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\psi)$ vanishes because\nopposite periodic faces cancel. The spatial vector-coupling term in the\nLagrangian is $q_{\\rm SI}c\\psi^\\dagger\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot\\mathbf A\\psi$,\nand the mass term is $-m_{\\rm SI}c^2\\psi^\\dagger\\beta\\psi$. The\nfirst-order canonical momenta are $i\\hbar\\psi^\\dagger/2$ and\n$-i\\hbar\\psi/2$, so their pairing with the time derivatives cancels the\ncanonical term. Negating the remaining Dirac Lagrangian terms in the Legendre\ntransform gives the matter Hamiltonian density\n$\\psi^\\dagger[c\\boldsymbol\\alpha\\cdot(-i\\hbar\\nabla-q_{\\rm SI}\\mathbf A)\n+\\beta m_{\\rm SI}c^2+q_{\\rm SI}\\varphi]\\psi$, whose scalar term is\n$\\varphi\\rho_q$. On stationary fields this source agrees with [F4].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For the finite-mode branch [A4], interpret the local density in [A1] on\nthe torus by its periodic lift and integrate over one spatial fundamental\ncell. Since $x^0=ct$, the physical Lagrangian is\n$L(t)=\\hbar c\\int_{\\mathbb T_L^3}\\mathcal L_{\\rm nat}\\,d^3\\mathbf x$.\nUsing $\\bar\\psi\\gamma^0=\\psi^\\dagger$, the part containing time\nderivatives of the spinor is\n$$\\frac{i\\hbar}{2}\\int_{\\mathbb T_L^3}(\\psi_N^\\dagger\\dot\\psi_N-\\dot\\psi_N^\\dagger\\psi_N)\\,d^3\\mathbf x.$$\nThe $A_0$ coupling and all spatial and mass terms contain no $\\dot z_j$.\nSubstitution of $\\psi_N=\\sum_jz_ju_j$ and the stated orthonormality give\nexactly $L_{\\rm kin}=\\frac{i\\hbar}{2}\\sum_j(\\bar z_j\\dot z_j-\\dot{\\bar z}_jz_j)$.\nIn the canonical normalization [F8], each $u_j$ has unit $\\mathrm m^{-3/2}$\nand each $z_j$ is dimensionless; since $[\\hbar]=\\mathrm{J\\,s}$ and\n$[\\dot z_j]=\\mathrm s^{-1}$, the displayed $L_{\\rm kin}$ has unit joule.\nThis restriction supplies the finite-mode kinetic term but does not assert\nthat the selected mode subspace is invariant under the field equations.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F7",
            "L2",
            "algebra",
            "A4",
            "F8",
            "F1",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "From [F1]–[F3], $S_0^2=\\mu_0\\hbar c$ and\n$F_{\\rm SI}=S_0f$, so the physical Maxwell Lagrangian density is\n$-F_{ab}^{\\rm SI}F_{\\rm SI}^{ab}/(4\\mu_0)$. The tensor conventions [F2]\ngive\n$F_{ab}^{\\rm SI}F_{\\rm SI}^{ab}=2(|\\mathbf B|^2-|\\mathbf E|^2/c^2)$;\nusing $\\varepsilon_0=(\\mu_0c^2)^{-1}$ from [F1], this becomes\n$\\mathcal L_M=\\varepsilon_0|\\dot{\\mathbf A}+\\nabla\\varphi|^2/2\n-|\\mathbf B|^2/(2\\mu_0)$. Differentiating with respect to\n$\\dot{\\mathbf A}$ gives\n$\\boldsymbol\\pi_A=\\varepsilon_0(\\dot{\\mathbf A}+\\nabla\\varphi)\n=-\\varepsilon_0\\mathbf E$; there is no $\\dot\\varphi$ term, so\n$\\pi_\\varphi=0$. Since\n$\\dot{\\mathbf A}=\\boldsymbol\\pi_A/\\varepsilon_0-\\nabla\\varphi$,\nthe Maxwell Legendre transform is\n$|\\boldsymbol\\pi_A|^2/(2\\varepsilon_0)+|\\mathbf B|^2/(2\\mu_0)\n-\\boldsymbol\\pi_A\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi$. Integrating the last term by\nparts on the torus gives $+\\varphi\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A$;\nopposite periodic faces cancel. Adding the matter Hamiltonian from step 1.1\ngives exactly the Hamiltonian in the Statement, including\n$\\varphi(\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A+\\rho_q)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "algebra",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Varying the unrestricted multiplier $\\varphi$ in step 2.1 imposes\n$\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol\\pi_A+\\rho_q=0$. Substituting\n$\\boldsymbol\\pi_A=-\\varepsilon_0\\mathbf E$ gives\n$\\varepsilon_0\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf E=\\rho_q$. In Coulomb gauge,\n$\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf E=-\\Delta\\varphi$ because\n$\\nabla\\cdot\\dot{\\mathbf A}=0$, so the constraint becomes\n$-\\Delta\\varphi=\\rho_q/\\varepsilon_0$. This is the Gauss sign in [F3].\nThe multiplier equation holds for every smooth periodic source; neutrality\nis needed only for a periodic solution.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Under the neutral-branch hypothesis in the Statement, the smoothness,\nperiodicity, and zero-mean hypotheses of [L1] hold at each $t\\in I$.\nIt supplies the unique zero-mean Fourier solution and energy identity. Each\nsummand is nonnegative because $\\varepsilon_0>0$ by [F1] and\n$|2\\pi\\mathbf n/L|^2>0$ for $\\mathbf n\\ne0$; hence the longitudinal\nenergy is nonnegative. This remains conditional on the current draft solver\nand Countable Choice and asserts no solution for nonneutral data.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "L1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "On the neutral branch [A3], [F1] and step 1.1 give\n$\\rho_q=-e_{\\rm SI}\\psi^\\dagger\\psi$ and\n$\\int_{\\mathbb T_L^3}\\psi^\\dagger\\psi\\,d^3\\mathbf x=0$ because\n$e_{\\rm SI}>0$. The integrand is continuous and nonnegative. If it were\npositive at any point, continuity would make it positive on a ball of\npositive volume, forcing a positive integral. Thus it vanishes everywhere\nand $\\psi=0$. The finite-mode calculation of step 1.2 is logically\nseparate and does not assert a solution in this neutral branch.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A3",
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "native-current-step-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "In the real canonical coordinates of the Statement,\n$\\{x_j,P^x_k\\}=\\{y_j,P^y_k\\}=\\delta_{jk}$ and the mixed coordinate-momentum\nbrackets vanish. Substitution of $z=x+iy$ and\n$p=(P^x-iP^y)/2$ therefore gives the displayed complex canonical brackets.\nWriting $L_{\\rm kin}=\\hbar\\sum_j(y_j\\dot x_j-x_j\\dot y_j)$ gives real\ncanonical momenta $P^x_j=\\hbar y_j$ and $P^y_j=-\\hbar x_j$, hence\n$p_j=i\\hbar\\bar z_j/2$ and $\\bar p_j=-i\\hbar z_j/2$. These are precisely\nthe primary constraints in the Statement. The verified finite-dimensional\nhypotheses of [L2] then give its displayed constraint matrix and inverse,\nthe second-class conclusion, and reduced bracket\n$\\{z_j,\\bar z_k\\}_D=-i\\delta_{jk}/\\hbar$. This is a commuting classical\ncoordinate calculation and does not derive fermionic CAR.",
          "inputs": [
            "L2",
            "A4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
